🐠 Population Genomics in Marine Organisms
Research Question

How do marine populations evolve, adapt, and diverge genetically across different environments despite remaining part of the same species?

🌊 Background

Marine organisms often occupy vast geographic ranges that span different environmental conditions. Ocean currents, temperature gradients, salinity, depth, and ecological barriers can all influence patterns of genetic variation.
Population genomics uses genome-wide data to investigate how populations exchange genes, become genetically differentiated, and adapt to local environments. Modern sequencing technologies allow researchers to examine thousands of genetic markers simultaneously, providing powerful insights into evolution and biodiversity.
Marine organisms are particularly useful for population genomic studies because they often have high dispersal potential yet still show evidence of local adaptation and population structure.


🧬 Why This Matters

Understanding genetic variation within marine populations is important for:
Evolutionary biology
Conservation genetics
Marine biodiversity research
Climate change adaptation studies
Fisheries management
Population resilience and sustainability

❓ Key Research Questions
Population Structure
How genetically connected are marine populations across different geographic regions?

Genetic Diversity
How much genetic variation exists within and between populations?

Adaptation
What genetic changes are associated with adaptation to different marine environments?

Gene Flow
How do ocean currents and dispersal influence the movement of genetic variation between populations?

Speciation
How do genetically distinct populations eventually become separate species?

